7.10.m:
They praised [him] with cherished words, bowing down with great reverence:
"Obeisance to the slayer of all enemies, and to the destroyer of the Dānavas;
obeisance to the unconquered god, to Vaikuṇṭha, to the great-souled one;
obeisance to him who has shaken off passion, and obeisance indeed to the True One!
Obeisance to the deity of the Sādhyas, obeisance to the Abode, to the wise Creator;
obeisance to Yama, to the god, and obeisance, obeisance to Jaya!
Obeisance to the son of Aditi, and to Nara-Nārāyaṇa;
obeisance to Sumati, and let obeisance be to Viṣṇu!
Obeisance to the one whose form is Vāmana, and to Kṛṣṇa Dvaipāyana;
obeisance to Rāma, to Rāma, and indeed obeisance to Dattātreya!
Obeisance to you, Narasiṃha, and obeisance, obeisance to the Creator;
obeisance to the Sādhya, to the Veda, and obeisance, obeisance indeed to the wielder of the discus!

Obeisance to the slayer of Śakuni, and obeisance to Dāmodara;
to him performing austerities in the water, and to him fond of the serpent-bed;
obeisance to the one whose form is Kapila, and to the Great Person;
obeisance to the one whose form is a rain-cloud, and to him dear to Mahādeva;
obeisance to the one whose form is half Rudra, and likewise obeisance to the one whose form is Umā;
to him who holds a discus and mace in his hand, and to the troop of Maheśvara!
And perpetual obeisance to Śipiviṣṭa, the bearer of the Śrīvatsa;
to Dhundhumāra, the hero, the slayer of Madhu and Kaiṭabha;
to the four-armed Kṛṣṇa, who bears the Kaustubha gem;
to Trivikrama standing in the sky, who is well-clad in yellow garments!

Obeisance to the slayer of men, the fierce wielder of the mace and sword;
to the yogin, the sacrificer, the destroyer of the wife of Bhṛgu!
To him whose form is the bull, the eternal, the foremost among the Ādityas;
to Cekitāna, the restrained, to Śauri, the kinsman of the Vṛṣṇis!
To the destroyer of Mura and Aśvagrīva, and likewise the slayer of the Asuras;
obeisance to you who have the bow Śārṅga, the destroyer of Saubha and Śālva!
Obeisance to you, Padmanābha, who enter the body of Brahmā;
obeisance to Jaya, to Śarva, and to him who has received boons from Rudra!
Obeisance to the Lord of All, the restorer of lost righteousness;
to the Person, the most choice, obeisance to you of a hundred arms!
By your grace may we cross beyond distress, O Supreme Person.
Deprived of our kingdom are we all; do you not know [this], O mighty-armed one?
[Deprived] by Hiraṇyākṣa, O Vaikuṇṭha—destroy him, O mighty-armed one!"
Sanatkumāra said:
Whatever mortal recites this Vaiṣṇava hymn filled with supreme devotion
is continually released from all sins.

Sanatkumāra said:
Then those tigers among the gods made his form:
[…]
the lords fashioned his body out of the seven worlds.
All the mountains became his bones, and rivers his channels;
the seven oceans were his blood; phlegm, bile, and wind were his qualities.
The herbs became his feces, and righteousness was made his semen;
his urine became the clouds, and the seven winds dwelling in the abdomen.
The Sādhyas became his head, and Rudra took his place on the crown;
on his forehead was righteousness, and his nose was the spirited Wind.
The sun and the moon were his eyes; the directions became his ears;
the vital breaths were on his brows, and day and night were his eyelashes.

Fire became his mouth, and the submarine fire rested within his jaws;
the mid-space was on his lip, and the serpents became his teeth.
His tusks were made as four very great missiles:
the Pāśupata missile first, and the second was the discus;
the third was the Brahmadaṇḍa, and the fourth the head of Brahmā;
Soma became his neck, and Rudra stood on his shoulder.
And on his cheeks likewise were the Ādityas, and his two front limbs were fashioned by the Rudras; / his fingers were Fame, Lakṣmī, and Siddhi as well.
[…] and outward activity; and his claws were all the weapons: / the staff of Yama, the staff of Time, and likewise the staff of Death,
and the staff of Rudra were made into the four leg-arms of the Lord; / the Sādhya gods were his chest, and his heart was Soma.

[He who is] the satisfactions and relatives, constantly deluding creatures with his māyā; / and within this immensely great Soul, all the principles are warp and woof,
all [principles] beginning with the puruṣa, and he who is pervaded by nothing; / without knowing whom there is no liberation, by whom this universe is bound;
by whom I was previously made the Prajāpati of all the worlds; / by whom you, the sacrifice, and Viṣṇu were created, O ancient one—
